solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of $a^2 + 9a + 24$ and $a + 4$.
First, we divide $a^2 + 9a + 24$ by $a + 4$ to get a remainder.

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & a + 4 & \\
\cline{2-3}
a^2 + 9a + 24 & a^2 & + 4a \\
\cline{2-3}
 & 0 & + 5a & + 24 \\
\cline{2-3}
 & & 5a & + 24 \\
\end{array}
\]

Since the remainder is $5a + 24$, we can now divide $a + 4$ by $5a + 24$.

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 5a + 24 & \\
\cline{2-3}
a + 4 & 5a & + 20 \\
\cline{2-3}
 & 0 & + 4 \\
\cline{2-3}
 & & 4 \\
\end{array}
\]

Since the remainder is $4$, we can now divide $5a + 24$ by $4$.

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 4 & \\
\cline{2-3}
5a + 24 & 5a & + 20 \\
\cline{2-3}
 & 0 & + 4 \\
\cline{2-3}
 & & 4 \\
\end{array}
\]

Since the remainder is $4$, we can now divide $4$ by the remainder.

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 4 & \\
\cline{2-3}
4 & 1 & \\
\cline{2-3}
 & 0 & \\
\end{array}
\]

Since the remainder is $0$, the GCD of $a^2 + 9a + 24$ and $a + 4$ is $\boxed{4}$.

The answer is: 4

