[/asy]

solution_steps:
Using the Binomial Theorem, we can write $(1 + i\sqrt{3})^{1990}$ as
\[(2\operatorname{cis} 60^\circ)^{1990} = 2^{1990} \operatorname{cis} (1990 \cdot 60^\circ).\]
By DeMoivre's Theorem, we have
\begin{align*}
(2\operatorname{cis} 60^\circ)^{1990} &= 2^{1990} \operatorname{cis} (1990 \cdot 60^\circ) \\
&= 2^{1990} \operatorname{cis} 120000^\circ \\
&= 2^{1990} \operatorname{cis} 240^\circ \\
&= 2^{1990} \left( -\frac{1}{2} - i \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) \\
&= -2^{1989} - 2^{1989} i \sqrt{3}.
\end{align*}

On the other hand, by the Binomial Theorem,
\begin{align*}
(1 + i\sqrt{3})^{1990} &= \binom{1990}{0} (i \sqrt{3})^0 + \binom{1990}{1} (i \sqrt{3})^1 + \binom{1990}{2} (i \sqrt{3})^2 + \dots + \binom{1990}{1990} (i \sqrt{3})^{1990} \\
&= \binom{1990}{0} + \binom{1990}{1} i \sqrt{3} + \binom{1990}{2} (-3) + \dots + \binom{1990}{1990} (-3)^{995}.
\end{align*}

Comparing real and imaginary parts, we get
\begin{align*}
\binom{1990}{0} + \binom{1990}{2} (-3) + \binom{1990}{4} (-3)^2 + \dotsb &= -2^{1989}, \\
\binom{1990}{1} \sqrt{3} + \binom{1990}{3} (-3) \sqrt{3} + \binom{1990}{5} (-3)^2 \sqrt{3} + \dotsb &= -2^{1989} \sqrt{3}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the answer is
\[\frac{1}{2^{1990}} \sum_{n = 0}^{995} (-3)^n \binom{1990}{2n} = \frac{-2^{1989}}{2^{1990}} = \boxed{-\frac{1}{2}}.\]The answer is: -\frac{1}{2}

